scene-render-1.1.xsd, scene-render-1.1.sch and capabilities-1.schema.json are byte-identical to these files. Local
XML filenames retain 1.1 for tooling compatibility. The native mirror of the Schematron (crates/sr-model/src/rules.rs)
implements every assert of that file, with the same ids and messages; the corpus (tests/corpus, tools/build_corpus.py)
has a case for every assert id, and the lxml oracle (tools/oracle.py) agrees with the manifest on every document. The
one known difference of test: sr-core's OCN7 leaves 'tracking' out of a text collider's shape parameters, which its
P3D6 and PYRO8 name (SREP 37: tracking changes the collider). The ocean builds its colliders' geometry once, so the
native OCN7 refuses an animated tracking on an ocean collider, as P3D6 and PYRO8 do; the Schematron accepts that
document (corpus case ocn7-text-tracking, a native-only rule case). It is an erratum of sr-core's OCN7 for an SREP to
settle. The warnings W05, W11 and W12 (SREP 76) and the program diagnostics are native only, as the SREPs define them.

Document version 1.8 (sr_model::SCHEMA_VERSION is "1.8") is the random-streams draft's, a local addition on this branch:
a 1.8 document is a 1.7 document whose simulations draw from the D24 streams of the draft's section 5
(crates/sr-model/tests/version_1_8.rs). Document version 1.7 is schema 1.7.0's. It adds no syntax and has no version
gate: SREP 87's scoping applies to documents of every version, and a 1.7 document validates and renders as the same
document at 1.6 (crates/sr-model/tests/version_1_7.rs). Document version 1.6 is schema 1.6.0's. A 1.6 document holding
1.5 content
validates and renders as the same document at 1.5; the version gates V12 to V16 (SREPs 66 and 69, 67, 70, 74 and 75)
refuse 1.6 syntax in versions 1.0 to 1.5 (GATES_1_6 in crates/sr-model/tests/version_1_6.rs and
crates/sr-eval/tests/scene/version_1_6.rs). BH1 (SREP 76), VOX1 (SREP 80) and SREP 40's gates V8, MSQ1, P3D1, OCN1,
CRT1, FRX1 and GEO1 (SREP 83) refuse only versions 1.0 to 1.2, so these elements are valid at 1.3 and later; SREP 86
(Process, Active) records this as the exception to SREP 0's versioning rule for blackHole, accretionDisk,
camera/@geodesics, pyroBlast, voxelAsset and voxel objects.

Implemented here, by SREP (sr-core 1.6.0 and 1.7.0):
- SREPs 66 and 69: build-time and stepping WebAssembly programs (V12, PRG1 to PRG3, program diagnostics).
- SREP 67: compute, iterate and tonemap, the effect types error-diffusion and segmented-sort (V13, ITR1, ITR2, CMP13,
  SRT1).
- SREP 68: effectType/@stepsPerFrame and @prewarm, and program[@mode='step'] (STP1).
- SREP 70: parametric paths, surfaces and heightfields (V14, PAR1, PAR2).
- SREP 71: subsurface materials, the procedural sky of a dome, generator-fed maps (SKY1, SKY2, MTX1-<map attribute>).
- SREP 72: shader content rectangles, supersampling, edge blending and float32 working textures; the information
  finding PREC1 for precision="f32" with 3D content (it replaces the engine's E23, sr-core kit case
  srep-0072-precision-3d-note) and the error PREC10 for a device without float32 blending or filtering. SREP 72's
  Scope lists object3D, particles3D, ocean, blackHole and accretionDisk as 3D content (blackHole and accretionDisk by
  SREP 72's erratum of 2026-10-11; SREP 72 is Final).
- SREP 73: flock/@orientToVelocity and the INERT-I16 diagnostic for streak flocks.
- SREP 74: viewport3D (V15, VP1, VP3); BH5 to BH8 and W11 look only in the camera's or hole's scope, the viewport3D it
  is in, else the document outside every viewport3D.
- SREP 75: the fractal asset (V16, FRC1). The model generator of sr-model gains the builtin xs:decimal, kept as its
  exact text. libxml2 (the oracle) refuses an xs:decimal of more than 24 digits, so the corpus keeps to 24.
- SREP 76: blackHole and accretionDisk, camera/@geodesics (BH1 to BH8, every xs:boolean form of geodesics and
  denoise); the warnings W11 (was W03) and W12 (was W04), W03 and W04 being SREP 57's generated-marker warnings; the
  geodesic pass divides the disk profile by its exact maximum, (6/7)^(3/2) 7^(-1/4).
- SREP 77: crater/@mantle, @bulking, @repose, fracture/@source, @minImpulse, @energyFraction, ocean/@density
  (CRT10 to CRT12, every xs:boolean form of mantle; FRX5 to FRX7).
- SREP 78: pyro/@follow, @followMargin, @followLoss and pyroBlast (PYRO9 to PYRO11, PYC5, PYC6).
- SREP 79: medium/@scatterBounces and the whitewater foam attributes (OCN14).
- SREP 80: voxel assets, voxel objects and bodies, fracture partitions (VOX1 to VOX15, CRT13 to CRT17, FRX8 to FRX15).
- SREP 81: the SREP 40 annotations and the rule message it takes upstream (documentation only).
- SREP 82: transition/@audio, the sound of video layers across a transition.
- SREP 83: SREP 40's version gates admit 1.3 or later; messages say "1.3 or later".
- SREP 84: C15, C43, R15, R48-from, R48-to, VOL5, C80, GEO2 and PYRO9 to PYRO11 read their xs:boolean attributes by
  value (normalize-space(@a)='true' or '1', 'false' or '0' for C15's reverse) and their integer attributes whatever
  their sign, leading zeros or padding; the native mirror uses xs_true, xs_false and xs_number, and W02 reads
  pyro/@follow the same way.
- SREP 85: C13, C14, C15, C37, C38, C44, C52, C53, C54, C56, PYRO1, PYRO2, PYRO4, VOL4, P3D3, P3D10, OCN3, OCN5,
  PYRO11, PYC6, FRX11 and, by its amendment of SREP 67, ITR1 and SRT1 read their xs:double, xs:decimal and integer
  attributes (and list items) by value, whatever their sign:
  number(substring(normalize-space(@a),1+number(starts-with(normalize-space(@a),'+')))) for number(@a), and the same
  with '0' appended for an absent attribute for sum(@a). The native mirror reads them with xs_number (through num());
  the warning W02 and OCN14's native reading take it too. Exponent forms (1e0): the native validator is normative for
  them (the SREP's Semantics 4) and xs_number reads them as libxml2 does. INF, -INF and NaN stay NaN, as in XPath,
  and each native rule compares them as its Schematron test does (OCN3's wavelength, OCN5, P3D3's direction and clay's
  boil and fingerprints let a NaN through; corpus cases *-inf, valid with W01). One verdict differs from v0.3.2: its
  native PYRO2 read pyroSource/@end and @start with Rust's parser, which reads INF as infinity, so end="INF" passed
  there while the Schematron refused it; PYRO2 now refuses it natively too (corpus case pyro2-end-inf).
- SREP 89: the supersample filter clamps each channel to [0, max(1, M)], M the largest sample of non-zero weight,
  instead of [0, 1] (SREP 72 Semantics 4 as amended); the XSD's documentation of project/@supersample says so.
- SREP 87 (schema 1.7.0, by the exception SREP 90 records): an id E that the build-mode program P emits is P.E in the
  document; inside an output, IDREF and IDREFS tokens, prop and propAtTime ids and the node id of link/@source resolve
  to the output's own ids first; a bare reference from outside (those positions, and override/@target of a layout, a
  variant, an instance or an include) resolves when exactly one program emitted the id, with the warning W13, and
  fails with PRG17 when several did; the bone names of an emitted skeleton's skin weights are its local ids; PRG14
  covers a repeated id within one output and a namespaced id equal to another id (sr-model program_ids.rs).

SREP 57 generated markers, SREP 59 reference checks and SREP 63 conditional capability entries are implemented. The
former pending SREPs 16, 17, 18, 19, 21 and 26 are implemented; PDF resolution requires the pdf build feature.

Unverified in this engine:
- SREP 67: real-GPU carry, because there is no discrete GPU we may use. The two-word 64-bit cells are tested on
  llvmpipe only, in CI and on the prod VM (raster_effects::srep67_compute). The error-diffusion kernel weights are
  checked against the SREP's table and against the commonly republished values, not against the original
  publications, which could not be read from here.
- SREP 71: the shape-parameter fit s(A) = 1.85 − A + 7|A − 0.8|³ and the profile are as the SREP states them; the
  SREP checked them against appleseed's implementation, not against Pixar Technical Memo 15-04, and neither was read
  from here. The subsurface lobe is a curvature pre-integration of the profile (sr_3d::subsurface), checked against a
  brute-force integral on a sphere and against the SREP's five properties; it is not compared with a random walk.
